WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.900
For the first time in decades, Virginia bars
and restaurants can finally advertise happy hour.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:10.940
It's the latest in a number of recent victories
that make it easier to enjoy your favorite drink.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:16.710
In 2019 we also saw the repeal of a law that
banned distilling spirits on Native American lands

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:21.430
and a supreme court decision that could open
up alcohol sales across state lines.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:26.640
The recent reforms we've seen in places like
Virginia with their happy hour law may seem small

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.280
but they are slowly pointing us towards a
better boozy future.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.660
We asked R Street's resident alcohol law expert
Jarret Dieterle

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.000
about some of the most nonsensical alcohol
laws.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:42.240
There's still a lot of dumb alcohol laws on
the books today and here's five examples of them.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.380
In North Carolina good luck getting a cheap
drink with buddies after work.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.280
North Carolina doesn't have happy hour at
all.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.220
I like to call them sad hours.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.040
You can get food specials, you can get discounts
on that,

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.300
but you can't get drink specials which is
what everyone associates happy hour with.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.660
They also have other weird restrictions like
buying your buddies a round.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:04.860
If you buy a pitcher of beer for example,
anything that has more than one serving of alcohol,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.820
two people have to go to the bar and pay for it,

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.700
one person can't just pay it and bring it back to the table.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:14.200
So it's pretty a fairly depressing place in
North Carolina when it comes five o clock

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.780
and you want to head to the bar.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.939
And then there's Utah's Zion Curtain.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.100
It requires bar tenders basically to stand
behind a partition or a wall

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.960
when they're mixing cocktails.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:28.680
The Utah legislature tried to reform it and
they ended up making it even more complicated.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:34.580
The justification for the law is they don't
want the children or people that are sensitive I guess

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:38.780
to see cocktails and hard alcohols being mixed
together.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:42.840
Every other state of course you can go into
and watch a bartender actually mixing a drink

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.120
and it's completely fine and normal and hurts
no one.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:50.299
And in Louisiana you can't have airplane sized
liquor bottles.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.260
Louisiana currently allows 100 milliliter
bottles

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.899
which are twice the size of the normal airplane bottles

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:01.180
but they still arbitrarily draw a limit on
the regular 50 milliliter size ones that we want.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:06.200
I think the theory for it originally is that
little liquor bottles are easier for people to conceal.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:11.080
That might allow people that have an addiction
issue to over indulge more

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.140
if they have a small concealable bottle.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.830
Of course lots of states have done the opposite

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.720
and thought that less amounts of alcohol

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.200
is probably better for people that have an addiction problem.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.420
In Indiana, good luck picking up a cold six
pack.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:27.000
Their gas stations cannot sell refrigerated
beer, they have to leave it at room temperature

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.220
and it has to be warm.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.340
You know, if your'e going in, filling up your
tank of gas,

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:32.970
and want to grab a chilled twelve pack you
can't do it.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:36.540
Which makes summer barbecues particularly
depressing I'm sure.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.930
The market's actually responded in a fairly
clever way

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:41.000
although they probably shouldn't have to

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.960
but some of the, you'll see in gas stations
in Indiana now

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.860
is like chiller bags that they'll fill with ice.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.940
Apparently they claim to, you know, chill
the beer in 15 minutes or really quickly.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.400
But again you have to ask yourself why we
created an arbitrary barrier

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:56.849
that forces something like that to even be
necessary

00:02:56.849 --> 00:02:58.900
whereas it isn't in other states.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:00.760
And finally, in some states,

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.640
government stores
are the only place to get your favorite bottle of bourbon.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.480
Some places like Virginia ABC stores,

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.440
you actually have to go to a retail store
run by the government of Virginia

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:13.160
to be able to buy your alcohol.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.860
People should care because it limits their
choices,

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:19.599
and it also hurts producers that are trying
to create innovative products.

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:23.909
So despite all the progress that's been made
in eliminating stupid liquor laws,

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:27.879
the rules governing the sector are still often
random and absurd.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:32.260
So let's raise a glass to the ongoing fight
to liberalizing alcohol sales.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:36.560
Because open markets mean colder, cheaper
and more abundant booze.

